## `dostoevsky/` 
- [[jesus-kiss-as-pre-theoretical-response]] — Jesus's silent kiss as a response from a different experiential register than argument
- [[the-unsolvable-as-productive-only-from-outside]] — Every thinker claims the unsolvable condition is generative, but the crush and Cioran's bind suggest this productivity is visible mainly in retrospect and from outside the condition itself
- [[obsessive-desire-as-self-destruction]] — Rogojin's passion as involuntary, bodily, potentially fatal force
- [[happiness-as-attention]] — Myshkin's Swiss happiness as a skill of perception, not a product of circumstance
- [[shame-internalized-as-identity]] — How social punishment produces internal collapse in the victim
- [[rescue-refused-by-the-rescued]] — Why Nastasia rejects the prince's genuine offer of salvation
## `nihilism/` 

- [[philosophical-responses-to-the-void]] — Existential thinkers differ less in whether they confront the void than in how they respond to it: through endurance, revolt, freedom, ethical reciprocity, or faith and active love.
## `ethics/` 

- [[camus-critique-of-philosophical-systems]] — Camus' argument that philosophical system-building is rooted in avoidance of suffering and proved helpless against fascism

## `selfhood/` 

- [[individuality-within-culture]] — True individuality as inhabiting and owning one's place in a culture
## `religion/` 

- [[sacred-and-profane-duality]] — The dualistic split between the divine and the worldly in Christian experience
- [[ideology-replaces-religion]] — How nationalism, Marxism, and other isms filled the structural role religion once played
## `schelling/`

- [[schelling-absolute-nothingness]] — Schelling's Unground as the groundless ground enabling existence to unfold
## `macintyre/`

- [[characters-of-emotivist-culture]] — The manager, therapist, and protester as dominant characters in a world without moral consensus
- [[emotivism-reinterpreted-as-theory-of-use]] — MacIntyre's reinterpretation: emotivism is false about meaning but accurate about how moral language functions in modern culture
- [[moores-principia-ethica]] — G.E. Moore's three claims: good as non-natural property, utilitarian ethics, and friendship/beauty as sole goods

## `beauvoir/` 

- [[ambiguity-of-human-existence]] — Human existence is fundamentally ambiguous: we live between multiple unresolvable dualities simultaneously
- [[maximizing-freedom-as-ethical-imperative]] — The ethical conclusion: orient actions toward expanding freedom rather than fleeing it
- [[why-the-adventurer-fails]] — The adventurer denies the individual/collective duality and ignores the web of freedom sustaining them
- [[the-aesthetic-attitude]] — The ideal of encountering art through detached, disinterested perception, as if one could suspend bias and see the work “as it is”
- [[situated-perception]] — Perception is always shaped by perspective, history, and embodiment, so claims to a neutral “view from nowhere” are deceptive
- [[the-aesthetic-attitude-and-scientific-inquiry]] — The aesthetic attitude resembles scientific inquiry in its attempt to suspend bias, but becomes dangerous when detachment hardens into false objectivity and evasion of responsibility
- [[how-to-live-in-ambiguity]] — A good human life and a valid ethics can be grounded in ambiguity through responsibility, shared freedom, and resistance to oppression.

## `cioran/`

- [[ciorans-critique-of-acceptance]] — Why Stoic-style acceptance is either performed rather than real, or insufficient to sustain a life
- [[nothingness-as-ciorans-central-premise]] — Nothingness as the starting point: loving absolute uselessness as a reason to go on
- [[cioran-on-suicide]] — Why Cioran rejected suicide despite his preoccupation with darkness and meaninglessness

## `byung-chul-han/`

- [[positive-power-vs-negative-power]] — Han's framework: negative power controls through prohibition; positive power controls through the rhetoric of limitless possibility
## `simone-weil/`

- [[affliction]] — Weil's term for the spiritual malaise and learned helplessness produced by dehumanizing conditions
- [[empathy-as-exploit-inverts-attention-as-sacrament]] — Synthesis of Weil's attention and Musk's "empathy as software exploit" as structurally antithetical responses to the same phenomenon, with Han's achievement society as the intermediary that made the inversion culturally plausible

## `anarchism/`

- [[free-association-and-voluntary-community]] — The principle that communities should be voluntary, not coerced
- [[government-as-domination]] — The argument that the state dominates rather than mediates between citizens
- [[futurism-and-the-techno-savior-critique]] — Bookchin's critique of those who defer ecological responsibility to hypothetical future technology

## `zizek/`

- [[the-gap-between-symbols-and-the-real]] — The permanent, irreducible gap between what symbols can explain and the full complexity of the Real
## `housing-policy/`

- [[amsterdam-housing-crisis-overview]] — Overview of Amsterdam's crisis: long waiting lists, sky-high prices, and a system that rewards insiders while locking out newcomers
- [[vereveningsfonds-amsterdams-equalizing-fund]] — Amsterdam's land-rent redistribution fund: how it worked, why it's empty, and what that means for affordable housing
- [[homelessness-is-a-housing-problem]] — Colburn and Aldern's systematic demolition of standard explanations: it's housing cost and vacancy, not poverty or drugs
## `abundance/`

- [[raj-chetty-on-mobility-as-product-of-place]] — Upward mobility declined from 92% to 50% across generations; place determines life outcomes
- [[lawn-sign-liberalism]] — Symbolically liberal, operationally conservative: the blue-state housing hypocrisy
- [[naders-raiders-and-environmental-law]] — How liberal reformers built an apparatus to sue the government — and its unintended consequences
- [[we-just-burned-it]] — The barbarism of combustion-based civilization from the future's perspective

## `techno-politics/`

- [[governance-by-ai-doges-endpoint]] — DOGE's logical terminus: replacing democratic deliberation with executable code

## `violence-and-aesthetics/`
- [[jigsaw-as-strict-patriarch]] — Jigsaw as tyrannical father figure who believes suffering is love and pain teaches

## `love-and-desire/`

- [[emptiness-after-desire-passes]] — The vacuum left behind when a powerful desire is finally relinquished
- [[love-bombing-through-stendhals-lens]] — How Stendhal's framework illuminates the cruelty of love bombing and withdrawal
- [[desire-to-be-desired]] — Lacan's observation that the primary aim in love is often to be desired rather than to desire
- [[vulnerability-to-manipulation-through-crushes]] — How the emotional dynamics of crushes create exploitable psychological vulnerabilities

## `voltaire/`

- [[will-to-live-as-irrational-compulsion]] — The old woman's observation that humans cling to existence without justification
- [[auto-da-fe-as-governance-by-superstition]] — Credentialed ceremonial killing as bureaucratic policy response to disaster
- [[freedom-as-commodity-in-candide]] — The ransom economy where liberation operates through market exchange
- [[optimism-and-pessimism-as-twin-inadequacies]] — Both philosophical systems unfalsifiable, both useless for action
## `propaganda/` 
- [[rootedness-not-introspection-against-shaped-preference]] — Against the propaganda model, introspection is recursive; thickening the rootedness the apparatus erodes is the partial counter available

## `epistemics/` 
- [[iterating-particular-and-universal-as-method]] — Against picking either pole, the trustworthy picture of the world emerges from the oscillation between myself/other, particular/universal, inside/outside, is/ought
## `phenomenology/` 
- [[diagnosis-clear-destination-unfamiliar]] — Husserl's bracket-drift diagnosis is compelling; the proposed exit through the epoché is so unfamiliar that even he seems to struggle, so I take the diagnosis and bracket the destination 
- [[default-settings-have-history]] — Husserl's historical detective work as method: defaults become visible as choices only by walking back the multi-era trajectory that produced them
